  "content": {
    "id": "p22-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Two buses are 1000 m apart and are travelling directly towards one another, the first travelling at 23 m/s and the second at 17 m/s. At the same time, an unbelievably fast fly leaves the front of the first bus travelling at 50 m/s. When the fly reaches the second bus, it instantly reverses direction and flies towards the first bus, then continues flying back and forth between the rapidly approaching buses. At the moment the buses collide, what is the total distance that the fly has covered?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$1250\\text{ m}$.\n\nThe buses meet after $25\\text{ s}$, so the fly travels at $50\\text{ m/s}$ for $25\\text{ s}$.",
      "worked": "The buses approach each other at their combined speed. The fly travels until they collide.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\ns_{\\text{relative}}&=23+17\\\\\ns_{\\text{relative}}&=40\\text{ m/s}\\\\\nt&=\\frac{1000}{40}\\\\\nt&=25\\text{ s}\\\\\nd_{\\text{fly}}&=50\\times25\\\\\nd_{\\text{fly}}&=1250\\text{ m}\n\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 68
  },
